  "headline": "🇺🇬 WHO CERTIFIES UGANDA EBOLA-FREE — AND THE US LEVEL 4 STILL DOESN'T BUDGE",
  "body": "The WHO confirmed this week that Uganda has cleared the 42-day monitoring period with no new transmission (Al Jazeera, 26 Aug 2026). Total: **20 confirmed infections, two deaths**, last case 21 June. Kampala had declared it over on 28 July, counting from an earlier date — hence the month's gap. Permanent Secretary Diana Atwine: \"Unless they have other reasons, they should definitely lift the restrictions off the country.\" **We read travel.state.gov directly this afternoon.** Uganda remains **Level 4 — Do Not Travel**, issued **4 June 2026**. Unchanged for 84 days. **What everyone is missing:** State's own advisory history says the 17 May move to Level 4 was made *\"due to the Ebola Bundibugyo Virus Disease outbreak in Ituri Province in the DRC.\"* The trigger was never Ugandan epidemiology. Ituri is now the epicentre of the largest Ebola outbreak ever recorded — **5,656 confirmed cases, 2,715 deaths as at 25 August**, still climbing (DRC government data cited by ECDC, via Al Jazeera, 26 Aug 2026). Kampala cannot clear a condition set 877km away.",
  "sowhat": "🎯 **So what:** stop treating the Level 4 as a countdown. Contract 2027 as though it holds, reweight allocation towards UK, EU, Gulf and EAC trade, and ask your US agents this week what their insurers do with a Level 4 destination.",
  "segments": [
   "city",
   "bush"
  ],
